The playbook, one fix per failure class:

```js
// 1. NEVER SETTLES → race it against a timeout (make the hang visible)
const withTimeout = (p, ms, label) =>
  Promise.race([
    p,
    new Promise((_, reject) =>
      setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('timeout: ' + label)), ms)),
  ]);
const user = await withTimeout(fetchUser(id), 5000, 'fetchUser');
// audit checklist: every wrapped callback has a resolve AND reject path;
// listeners added BEFORE the event can fire; the promise isn't dropped.

// 2. RACE — stale response wins → epoch token + cancellation
let searchEpoch = 0;
input.addEventListener('input', async (e) => {
  const my = ++searchEpoch;                    // this request's ticket
  const ctrl = new AbortController();          // and its cancel handle
  currentCtrl?.abort();                        // cancel the PREVIOUS run
  currentCtrl = ctrl;
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/search?q=' + e.target.value, { signal: ctrl.signal });
    if (my !== searchEpoch) return;            // stale: a newer run started
    renderResults(await res.json());           // only NEWEST data renders
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') return;      // cancelled — not an error
    showError(err);                             // real errors surface
  }
});

// 3. SWALLOWED ERRORS → no floating promises; catch at the ROOT
async function save() {
  try {
    await validate();
    await persist();                 // awaited — errors reach THIS catch
  } catch (err) {
    report(err);                     // rethrow or handle — never silence
    throw new Error('save failed: ' + err.message, { cause: err });
  }
}
// global safety net during dev:
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (e) => {
  telemetry.error(e.reason);         // find the floating promise's origin
});
```

The diagnostic discipline: FIRST classify which of the three it is (never settles / wrong order / missing error), because the fixes differ — a timeout can't fix a race, and cancellation can't fix a swallowed catch. Then instrument boundaries: log the state (pending/settled) and the epoch before and after each await; DevTools async stack traces connect the throw site to the awaiting frame (the 'where did this come from' answer for async).

The prevention patterns worth naming: ALWAYS wire both resolve and reject in hand-rolled promise wrappers (the callback-API-with-no-error-path is the #1 never-settles source); sequence with a queue when order IS the requirement; and the { cause } option chains the original error — the modern replacement for string-concatenating error messages.
